Description: harden encoder slice poll loops against poll failure
 The split-output slice poll loops in the eight vepu5xx encoder HALs assign
 the MPP_DEV_CMD_POLL return value but never test it, and terminate only on a
 last flag read out of the returned slice records. The four h264e HALs also
 leave that flag uninitialised. When the kernel reports an error with
 count_ret == 0 the loop makes no progress and spins forever, which is what an
 aggressive H.264 low-delay split does once the RKVENC2 driver drops the
 terminal slice record from its 256-entry FIFO.
 .
 Each loop now consumes the returned records first, so a poll that delivers
 the terminal record together with an error still completes the frame, and
 only then fails with MPP_ERR_VPUHW. Consecutive polls that succeed without
 returning a record are bounded, so no driver behaviour can hang the loop. In
 seven of the eight HALs the statement after the loop is an unconditional
 status-register check that would otherwise overwrite the error, so it is
 gated behind the poll result. On the error path a terminal ENC_OUTPUT_FINISH
 callback is emitted when the loop is abandoned mid-frame, because that
 callback is the only thing that ends the packet for a low-delay consumer.
 .
 Seven of the HALs gate that terminal callback on their own per-frame
 slice_last. hal_h265e_vepu580.c ends its loop on a tile count rather than on
 slice_last, so it tracks delivery in a new per-frame finish_sent flag. It
 must not test ctx->output_cb->cmd: output_cb is the encoder-wide MppCbCtx and
 its cmd field persists across frames, so a stale ENC_OUTPUT_FINISH left by an
 earlier frame would suppress the callback and hang the consumer, which is the
 exact failure this patch exists to prevent.
 .
 The poll_type and poll_ret fields of MppDevPollCfg stay unused; this change
 needs no ABI or struct modification.
